content.text:
Assign the switched value to `never` in `default`:

```ts
  showNavBarUnitButton(): boolean {
    const mode = this.tcs.booklet?.config.navbar_unit_label;

    switch (mode) {
    case 'HIDDEN':
      return false;
    case 'INDEX':
    case 'LABEL':
      return true;
    default: {
      const exhaustiveCheck: never = mode;
      return exhaustiveCheck;
    }
    }
  }
```

Important: `mode` must be a narrowed union only. If optional chaining makes it include `undefined`, TypeScript may complain because `undefined` is not handled. Then add:

```ts
    if (!mode) {
      return false;
    }
```

before the switch, or avoid optional chaining if booklet is guaranteed there.

No. Don’t call it from the template if you care about repeated execution. Angular may re-evaluate template functions on each change detection pass.

Better: compute once when relevant state changes, store boolean.

```ts
showUnitNavigation = false;
```

Where you already update `unitNavContext`:

```ts
const navbarUnitLabel = this.tcs.booklet?.config.navbar_unit_label;
this.showUnitNavigation = this.getShowUnitNavigation(navbarUnitLabel);
```

Helper stays exhaustive:

```ts
private getShowUnitNavigation(
  navbarUnitLabel: 'HIDDEN' | 'INDEX' | 'LABEL' | undefined
): boolean {
  switch (navbarUnitLabel) {
  case undefined:
  case 'HIDDEN':
    return false;
  case 'INDEX':
  case 'LABEL':
    return true;
  default: {
    const exhaustiveCheck: never = navbarUnitLabel;
    return exhaustiveCheck;
  }
  }
}
```

Template:

```html
@if (showUnitNavigation && testLoaded) {
  <tc-navigation-control ...>
  </tc-navigation-control>
}
```

Even better: make `unitNavContext` nullable and use that as the display state. Then no duplicate boolean.
